(p/'README.md').write_text('# Actual original coprime ideal products and all-four class relation\n\nFive new modules extend all 1,192 exact prior sources. Universally prove I_m I_n=I_mn for actual original coordinate-kernel ideals when mn divides210 and explicit Bezout coefficients are supplied. Transport the literal equality into the full native O_K. Prove I_210=(theta) directly, then supply actual pair/triple products and the all-four principal product.\n\nApplying the native ClassGroup.mk0 map gives the genuine relation c2*c3*c5*c7=1 and c7=c2*c3*c5. It is derived from the actual principal ideal product, not assumed as a class relation or inferred from analytic labels. This prepares the upper bound by three two-torsion generators, but generation of the full class group, eight-element bijection and class-number exhaustion remain separate. No CM reciprocity or unramified-extension claim.\n\nNo local Lean or new resources; fresh distinct source review and kernel replay pending.\n')
(p/'DERIVATION.md').write_text('# Exact product identities before group relations\n\nFor z in I_m and w in I_n, write their real parts m*a and n*c and210=mn*q. Their product has real part mn*(a*c-q*im(z)*im(w)), so it lies in I_mn. Native Ideal.mul_le makes this universal over the full product ideal.\n\nFor the reverse inclusion use the already proved actual two-generator presentation I_mn=(mn,theta). The generator mn is the product of the two integer generators. The products m*theta and theta*n lie in I_m I_n; an explicit integer Bezout equation A*m+C*n=1 combines them to theta. Thus both inclusions are produced, with all hypotheses supplied concretely in the actual table.\n\nApply the exact full integer-ring map to this identity. Native Ideal.map_mul and the proved kernel-map identity yield the same product formula in the actual O_K. No index comparison replaces ideal equality.\n\nFor d=210 the kernel consists of elements210*a+s*theta. Each equals theta*(s-a*theta), using theta squared=-210; conversely theta is in the kernel. Therefore I_210 is the actual principal ideal(theta). Combine products2*3,6*5,30*7 with supplied Bezout pairs(-1,1),(1,-1),(4,-17) to prove the full four-prime product principal. Additional pair products2*5 and3*5 are supplied for the later independent-class lower bound.\n\nFinally use the actual nonzero-ideal monoid and ClassGroup.mk0 multiplicativity, with native mk0_eq_one_iff, to derive the all-four relation. The already produced class squares make inverses equal themselves, so the fourth class is the product of the first three. Full class-group generation still requires the native Minkowski prime-ideal classification described separately in the non-admitted recovery plan.\n')
(p/'regress_cm_prime_products_rebuild.py').write_text("from pathlib import Path\nfrom itertools import product\nimport json\nrows=[(2,3,-1,1),(2,5,-2,1),(3,5,2,-1),(6,5,1,-1),(30,7,4,-17)]\ncases=0\nfor m,n,A,C in rows:\n assert 210%(m*n)==0 and A*m+C*n==1\n q=210//(m*n)\n for a,b,c,d in product(range(-5,6),repeat=4):\n  assert (m*a*n*c-210*b*d)==m*n*(a*c-q*b*d)\n  cases+=1\nfor a,s in product(range(-100,101),repeat=2):\n assert (-210*(-a),s)==(210*a,s)\nassert 2*3*5*7==210\n# Omitting coprimality does not give theta from the Bezout combination.\nassert all(2*a+2*c!=1 for a,c in product(range(-10,11),repeat=2))\nout={'status':'PASS','product_coordinate_cases':cases,'exact_Bezout_pairs':5,'theta_divisibility_cases':201**2,'all_four_product':210,'negative_coprimality_control':1,'scope':'Exact regression only, not Lean or class-group exhaustion.'}\nPath(__file__).with_name('REGRESSION.json').write_text(json.dumps(out,indent=2)+'\\n');print(out)\n")
